      "text": "To determine how many integers Josef can pick such that Timothy's quotient is an integer when 1000 is divided by that integer, we need to consider the conditions under which the division results in an integer. According to the problem, Josef picks an integer \\( n \\) between 1 and 1000 inclusive, and Timothy divides 1000 by \\( n \\) and states whether the result is an integer or not. This means \\( n \\) must be a divisor of 1000.\n\nFirst, we need to find all the divisors of 1000. To do this, we first find the prime factorization of 1000:\n\\[\n1000 = 10^3 = (2 \\cdot 5)^3 = 2^3 \\cdot 5^3\n\\]\nThe formula to find the number of divisors of a number \\( n = p_1^{e_1} \\cdot p_2^{e_2} \\cdot \\ldots \\cdot p_k^{e_k} \\) is \\( (e_1 + 1)(e_2 + 1) \\ldots (e_k + 1) \\). Applying this to \\( 1000 = 2^3 \\cdot 5^3 \\), we get:\n\\[\n(3+1)(3+1) = 4 \\cdot 4 = 16\n\\]\nSo, 1000 has 16 divisors. These divisors are:\n\\[\n1, 2, 4, 5, 8, 10, 20, 25, 40, 50, 100, 125, 200, 250, 500, 1000\n\\]\nAll these divisors are integers, and none of them is the number 1000 itself (since 1000 divided by any of its divisors except 1000 would result in a quotient that is not an integer). Therefore, all 15 divisors (excluding 1000) are valid choices for Josef to pick.\n\nThus, the number of integers Josef can pick such that Timothy's quotient is an integer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{15}\n\\]",
