      "text": "To determine how many integers Josef can pick such that Timothy's quotient is an integer, we need to identify all the divisors of 1000. An integer \\( d \\) is a divisor of 1000 if and only if \\( 1000 \\div d \\) results in an integer.\n\nFirst, we find the prime factorization of 1000:\n\\[\n1000 = 10^3 = (2 \\times 5)^3 = 2^3 \\times 5^3\n\\]\n\nNext, we determine the number of divisors of 1000 using its prime factorization. If a number \\( n \\) has a prime factorization of the form \\( p_1^{e_1} \\times p_2^{e_2} \\times \\cdots \\times p_k^{e_k} \\), then the number of positive divisors of \\( n \\) is given by:\n\\[\n(e_1 + 1)(e_2 + 1) \\cdots (e_k + 1)\n\\]\n\nFor \\( 1000 = 2^3 \\times 5^3 \\), the exponents are 3 and 3. Therefore, the number of divisors is:\n\\[\n(3 + 1)(3 + 1) = 4 \\times 4 = 16\n\\]\n\nThus, the number of integers between 1 and 1000 (inclusive) that are divisors of 1000 is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{16}\n\\]",
